\section{An Array's Access Contract Determines Its Implementation}

An inferred memory is a match between RTL behavior and the resources in a selected target. Its important properties include width, depth, port count, clocks, enables, initialization, and what a read does during an address collision. Matching capacity is necessary but not sufficient. A device with synchronous block RAM cannot generally use it as a combinational lookup table without changing latency. LUT storage, flip-flops with muxes, replication, banking or an explicit external memory interface are different implementation choices, each with a different cost.

Sung, Kim, and Ha study \emph{software} synthesis from synchronous dataflow. Their examples separate the code size of a schedule from the capacity of buffers induced by it. In hardware, the analogous question is how many values are simultaneously live and how many accesses the schedule needs. For example, if producer \(P\) emits two words per firing and consumer \(C\) takes three, one balanced period requires three \(P\) and two \(C\) firings. With atomic firings and an initially empty buffer, \(P,P,P,C,C\) peaks at six words, while \(P,P,C,P,C\) peaks at four. This calculation gives capacity for that schedule, not port bandwidth or a block RAM count.

