\section{Specify Read-During-Write Before Counting Blocks}

The SystemVerilog module below \cite{ieee1800systemverilog} has one synchronous write port and one synchronous read port, with a common clock. Reset belongs to the surrounding protocol; the array is not cleared by hardware reset.

\begin{verbatim}
module store256(input logic clk, we, re,
                input logic [7:0] wa, ra,
                input logic [15:0] din,
                output logic [15:0] dout);
    logic [15:0] mem [0:255];
    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        if (we) mem[wa] <= din;
        if (re) dout <= mem[ra];
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Pseudocode for the read-first contract on a rising edge:

\begin{verbatim}
old = memory[ra] if re else unused    # take the pre-edge value
if we: memory[wa] = din               # commit the write
if re: dout = old                     # hold the prior output otherwise
\end{verbatim}

If address \(9\) contains \(17\), then a simultaneous read of \(9\) and write of \(42\) to \(9\) gives \(17\); a later enabled read gives \(42\) until another write. The simulator implements that ordering through nonblocking assignments. An implementation with a different native collision policy needs extra logic where possible or a different memory resource; silently promising write-first is not legal. A proof that the controller never reads and writes the same address could weaken the external contract, but document and verify the proof before asking inference to exploit it.

\Cref{fig:visual-316} shows why a capacity calculation does not settle port compatibility. In this case \(256\times16=4096\) payload bits, but the selected primitive still has to support this geometry and read policy.

