\section{Inspect Inference Before and After Mapping}

With the source saved as \texttt{store256.sv}, the following Yosys script preserves a collected memory for diagnosis instead of intentionally mapping it to generic flip-flops:

\begin{verbatim}
read_verilog -sv store256.sv
hierarchy -check -top store256
proc
memory -nomap
stat
write_rtlil store256.rtlil
\end{verbatim}

The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/memory.html}{Yosys memory documentation} describes collecting ports, merging a registered read into the memory representation where possible, selecting target-specific cells, and lowering leftover memories to generic logic. Follow the separate target synthesis flow and inspect its mapped cell inventory; seeing a memory in RTLIL does not prove that the design uses block RAM. Per-bit write enables, arbitrary clocks, and a one-cycle clear of the entire array can rule out the primitive. Valid bits or a sequential initialization pass can solve some reset requirements, but the latter changes startup latency.

\section{Preserve Arithmetic the DSP Block Can Recognize}

Kasprzyk and colleagues note that bit-width analysis is useful for hardware realization. Width and signedness matter especially for multiply-accumulate inference. For example the SystemVerilog fragment \verb|logic signed [15:0] a, b;| with \verb|logic signed [31:0] p;| and \verb|always_ff @(posedge clk) p <= a * b;| represents a full-width signed product. The case \(a=b=-32768\) produces \(2^{30}\), one more than the maximum of a signed 31-bit result, so dropping a bit changes the contract.

Targets differ in multiplier widths, pre-adders, accumulator modes and staging registers; an unsupported combination may use several DSP blocks, a DSP plus fabric, or only soft logic. A gratuitous truncation or an incompatible reset can stop a multiply-plus-add from fitting one native block. Conversely, forcing DSP inference for a small constant operator can waste a scarce block. Preserve word-level arithmetic until the target pass has a chance to see it, and check the implementation report for the final resource tradeoff. A successful inference alone says nothing about a routed clock period.
